I now have a complete picture. Here are my findings.

## Overview

This is the Spring PetClinic REST sample. Owner creation flows through `POST /api/owners`, implemented in the V1 controller's `addOwner` method. The codebase already implements several 409-Conflict rules (duplicate telephone, duplicate household, city capacity) but does NOT yet implement email uniqueness — and there is a failing acceptance test expecting exactly that.

## 1. Owner entity, repository, service, controller

- Entity: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`
  - `@Entity @Table(name = "owners")`, extends `Person` (lines 36-38). Has an `email` field with NO uniqueness constraint (lines 52-53): `@Column(name = "email") private String email;` — note it is not `@NotEmpty`, so email is optional.
  - Bean-validation constraints: `address` and `city` are `@NotEmpty`; `telephone` is `@NotEmpty` + `@Pattern(regexp = "^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$", ...)` (lines 39-50).
- Repository interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`
- Repository implementations (three arms): 
  - `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`
  - `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`
  - `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java`
- Service interface: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java` (owner methods at lines 57-101)
- Service impl: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java` (owner methods at lines 247-306)
- Controllers:
  - V1 (the one with the POST create): `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`
  - V2 (only a paged list endpoint, no create): `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v2/OwnerRestControllerV2.java`
- Mapper: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` (MapStruct; `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` at line 38 maps `email` field-by-name automatically).

## 2. The POST create endpoint

`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`, lines 134-172. Verbatim:

```java
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")
    @Override
    public ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto) {
        ownerFieldsDto.setAddress(AddressNormalizer.normalize(ownerFieldsDto.getAddress()));
        List<String> missingFields = OwnerFieldsValidator.findMissingFields(ownerFieldsDto);
        if (!missingFields.isEmpty()) {
            throw new MissingOwnerFieldsException(missingFields);
        }
        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);
        if (this.clinicService.countOwnersByCity(owner.getCity()) >= MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY) {
            throw new CityCapacityExceededException(owner.getCity());
        }
        LocalDate registrationDate = RegistrationDatePolicy.effectiveDate(owner.getRegistrationDate());
        if (this.clinicService.countOwnersRegisteredOn(registrationDate) >= MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY) {
            throw new DailyOwnerLimitExceededException(registrationDate);
        }
        String telephone = TelephoneNormalizer.normalize(owner.getTelephone());
        if (!this.clinicService.findOwnerByTelephone(telephone).isEmpty()) {
            throw new DuplicateTelephoneException(telephone);
        }
        Collection<Owner> household =
            this.clinicService.findOwnersInHousehold(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress());
        boolean sharesHousehold = Boolean.TRUE.equals(ownerFieldsDto.getSharesHousehold());
        if (!household.isEmpty() && !sharesHousehold) {
            throw new DuplicateHouseholdException(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress());
        }
        owner.setTelephone(telephone);
        owner.setEmail(EmailNormalizer.normalize(owner.getEmail()));
        if (sharesHousehold) {
            this.clinicService.joinHousehold(owner, household);
        }
        this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
        this.ownerAuditor.ownerCreated(owner);
        OwnerDto ownerDto = toOwnerDtoWithBulkSignupWarning(owner);
        headers.setLocation(UriComponentsBuilder.newInstance()
            .path("/api/owners/{id}").buildAndExpand(owner.getId()).toUri());
        return new ResponseEntity<>(ownerDto, headers, HttpStatus.CREATED);
    }
```

Constants at lines 72/78/85: `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 50`, `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100`, `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 80`. Note the ordering of validation checks: missing-fields → city capacity → daily limit → duplicate telephone → duplicate household. Email is normalized (line 162) but never checked for uniqueness.

The actual HTTP path binding (`POST /api/owners`) comes from the generated `OwnersApi` interface that `OwnerRestControllerV1` implements (`org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.api.OwnersApi`, generated from OpenAPI; `@RequestMapping("/api")` is on the controller at line 65).

## 3. Existing validation / exception handling incl. 409

Validation package: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/`

- `OwnerFieldsValidator.java` — required fields are firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone (email NOT required); `findMissingFields` at lines 53-62.
- Exceptions mapped to 409 CONFLICT:
  - `DuplicateTelephoneException.java` (RuntimeException, holds `telephone`)
  - `DuplicateHouseholdException.java` (RuntimeException, holds `lastName`, `address`)
  - `CityCapacityExceededException.java`
- Other: `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException.java` (→ 429), `MissingOwnerFieldsException.java` (→ 400), `InvalidTelephoneException.java` (→ 400).
- Normalizers used during create: `AddressNormalizer`, `TelephoneNormalizer`, `EmailNormalizer`.

There is currently NO email-uniqueness exception or check anywhere (grep for `findByEmail`/email in the repository and mapper returned nothing).

## 4. Owner repository interface methods

`/home/daniel/sandbox/.../repository/OwnerRepository.java`:
- `Collection<Owner> findByLastName(String)` (line 47) and paged `findByLastName(String, Pageable)` (line 49)
- `Collection<Owner> findByTelephone(String)` (line 58)
- `Collection<Owner> findByLastNameIgnoreCase(String)` (line 68) — used for household matching
- `Owner findById(int)` (line 77)
- `void save(Owner)` (line 86)
- `Collection<Owner> findAll()` (line 94) and paged `findAll(Pageable)` (line 96)
- `long count()` (line 103)
- `long countByCity(String)` (line 111)
- `long countByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)` (line 119)
- `void delete(Owner)` (line 127)

There is NO `findByEmail` method. Adding one (implemented across all three repository arms) is what an email-uniqueness rule would require.

## 5. Existing tests for owner creation

The only tests in the repo are under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/`:

- `AcceptanceTest.java` (lines 10-18) — the sole test, currently asserting email uniqueness, verbatim:

```java
	@Test
	void coreRejectsDuplicateEmail() throws Exception {
		ObjectNode a = ownerNode();
		a.put("email", uniqueEmail());
		createOwnerOk(a);
		ObjectNode b = ownerNode();
		b.put("email", a.get("email").asText());
		createOwner(b).andExpect(status().isConflict());
	}
```

The class comment (line 7) states the requirement: "email-unique: Reject creating an owner whose lower-cased email is already used by any other owner." This test expects `POST /api/owners` with a duplicate (lower-cased) email to return 409 Conflict — behavior NOT yet implemented in `addOwner`.

- `AcceptanceBase.java` — black-box test harness (`@SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc @Transactional @WithMockUser(roles OWNER_ADMIN...)`). Key helpers: `ownerNode()` (fully-unique valid owner, lines 117-125), `createOwner`/`createOwnerOk` posting to `/api/owners` (lines 155-162), `uniqueEmail()` (lines 106-108), `getOwner`/`fetchOwner`. Note: `ownerNode()` does not include an email by default.
- `AuditLogCapture.java` — test support for audit assertions.

## 6. Exception-to-HTTP-status mapping

Single global handler: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`, annotated `@ControllerAdvice` (line 55). It builds RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` responses via `detailBuild` (lines 75-83). Handlers:

- `AccessDeniedException` → re-thrown for Spring Security (lines 94-97)
- `Exception` → 500 (lines 106-113)
- `DataIntegrityViolationException` → 404 (lines 123-134)
- `MethodArgumentNotValidException` → 400 (lines 143-179)
- `MissingOwnerFieldsException` → 400 (lines 190-197)
- `InvalidTelephoneException` → 400 (lines 207-214)
- `DuplicateTelephoneException` → 409 CONFLICT (lines 224-231), sets `errors: ["telephone"]`
- `DuplicateHouseholdException` → 409 CONFLICT (lines 242-249), sets `errors: ["lastName","address"]`
- `CityCapacityExceededException` → 409 CONFLICT (lines 259-266), sets `errors: ["city"]`
- `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException` → 429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS (lines 277-284)

There are no `@ResponseStatus` annotations on the exception classes themselves; status is assigned entirely by these `@ExceptionHandler` methods. To implement the failing email-uniqueness test you would follow the exact pattern of `DuplicateTelephoneException` — a new exception + a new `@ExceptionHandler` returning `HttpStatus.CONFLICT`, plus a lookup (e.g. a `findByEmail`/`findOwnerByEmail`) and a check in `addOwner`.

